The implementation has two files:

`token_bucket.go` — the rate limiter itself:
- `NewTokenBucket(burstSize, refillRate)` creates a bucket that starts full.
- `Allow()` / `AllowN(n)` atomically check and consume tokens, returning whether the request is permitted.
- Refill is lazy — tokens are calculated from elapsed time on each call rather than using a background ticker, which avoids goroutine leaks and keeps the implementation simple.
- A `sync.Mutex` protects all mutable state for safe concurrent use.

`token_bucket_test.go` covers consumption, refill over time, the burst cap, and a concurrent stress test that verifies exactly 100 out of 200 goroutines succeed with a 100-token bucket and zero refill.

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define POOL_ALIGNMENT 8

typedef struct mem_pool {
    void *memory;          /* backing memory region */
    void *free_list;       /* head of free block linked list */
    size_t block_size;     /* size of each block (aligned) */
    size_t block_count;    /* total number of blocks */
    size_t blocks_used;    /* number of currently allocated blocks */
} mem_pool_t;

/**
 * Align a size up to the nearest multiple of POOL_ALIGNMENT.
 */
static size_t align_up(size_t size, size_t alignment) {
    return (size + alignment - 1) & ~(alignment - 1);
}

/**
 * Initialize a memory pool.
 *
 * @param pool        Pointer to pool structure to initialize.
 * @param block_size  Requested size of each block in bytes.
 *                    Will be rounded up to satisfy alignment and minimum size.
 * @param block_count Number of blocks in the pool.
 * @return 0 on success, -1 on failure.
 */
int pool_init(mem_pool_t *pool, size_t block_size, size_t block_count) {
    if (!pool || block_size == 0 || block_count == 0) {
        return -1;
    }

    /* Each free block stores a pointer, so enforce a minimum size.
     * Then align to 8 bytes for 64-bit compatibility. */
    size_t min_size = sizeof(void *);
    if (block_size < min_size) {
        block_size = min_size;
    }
    block_size = align_up(block_size, POOL_ALIGNMENT);

    /* Allocate the backing memory with proper alignment */
    void *memory = NULL;
    if (posix_memalign(&memory, POOL_ALIGNMENT, block_size * block_count) != 0) {
        return -1;
    }

    pool->memory = memory;
    pool->block_size = block_size;
    pool->block_count = block_count;
    pool->blocks_used = 0;

    /* Build the free list by threading a singly-linked list through the blocks.
     * Each free block's first sizeof(void*) bytes hold a pointer to the next free block. */
    pool->free_list = memory;
    uint8_t *current = (uint8_t *)memory;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < block_count - 1; i++) {
        uint8_t *next = current + block_size;
        *(void **)current = next;
        current = next;
    }
    *(void **)current = NULL; /* last block points to nothing */

    return 0;
}

/**
 * Allocate a single block from the pool. O(1).
 *
 * @param pool Pointer to an initialized pool.
 * @return Pointer to the allocated block, or NULL if the pool is exhausted.
 */
void *pool_alloc(mem_pool_t *pool) {
    if (!pool || !pool->free_list) {
        return NULL;
    }

    /* Pop the head of the free list */
    void *block = pool->free_list;
    pool->free_list = *(void **)block;
    pool->blocks_used++;

    return block;
}

/**
 * Return a block to the pool. O(1).
 *
 * @param pool  Pointer to the pool the block belongs to.
 * @param block Pointer previously returned by pool_alloc.
 */
void pool_free(mem_pool_t *pool, void *block) {
    if (!pool || !block) {
        return;
    }

    /* Basic bounds check: ensure the pointer actually belongs to this pool */
    uint8_t *start = (uint8_t *)pool->memory;
    uint8_t *end = start + (pool->block_size * pool->block_count);
    uint8_t *ptr = (uint8_t *)block;

    if (ptr < start || ptr >= end) {
        return; /* not our block */
    }

    /* Verify alignment to block boundary */
    if (((uintptr_t)(ptr - start)) % pool->block_size != 0) {
        return; /* misaligned pointer, not a valid block start */
    }

    /* Push onto the front of the free list */
    *(void **)block = pool->free_list;
    pool->free_list = block;
    pool->blocks_used--;
}

/**
 * Destroy the pool and release backing memory.
 */
void pool_destroy(mem_pool_t *pool) {
    if (!pool) {
        return;
    }
    free(pool->memory);
    pool->memory = NULL;
    pool->free_list = NULL;
    pool->block_count = 0;
    pool->blocks_used = 0;
}
```

Key design points:

- O(1) alloc and free — both are a single linked-list head pop/push. No searching or coalescing.
- Alignment — block sizes are rounded up to 8-byte boundaries, and `posix_memalign` ensures the base allocation is also aligned. This satisfies natural alignment for any 64-bit primitive.
- Free list is intrusive — free blocks store the next-pointer inside their own memory, so there's zero metadata overhead per block.
- Safety checks in `pool_free` — bounds checking and alignment verification prevent returning garbage pointers to the pool. This catches the most common misuse without adding per-block bookkeeping.
- The minimum block size is `sizeof(void*)` (8 bytes on 64-bit) since each free block must hold the intrusive list pointer.

