A couple of thoughts on your revised pattern:

The `{ once: true }` option is the right call for listener cleanup. But you still have the same fundamental issue: if the timeout fires and `fn` starts executing, then the signal gets aborted (e.g., by a new call superseding this one), the `abort` event listener will fire and call `reject`, while the `catch` block inside the `setTimeout` callback will also call `reject`. Same race, just slightly reorganized.

To truly eliminate the double-settle, you'd gate everything behind a `settled` flag:

```javascript
return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  let settled = false;

  const settle = (fn) => {
    if (!settled) {
      settled = true;
      fn();
    }
  };

  timeoutId = setTimeout(async () => {
    timeoutId = null;
    if (signal.aborted) {
      settle(() => reject(new DOMException('Debounced call was cancelled', 'AbortError')));
      return;
    }

    try {
      const result = await fn(...args, signal);
      settle(() => signal.aborted
        ? reject(new DOMException('Debounced call was cancelled', 'AbortError'))
        : resolve(result)
      );
    } catch (err) {
      settle(() => signal.aborted
        ? reject(new DOMException('Debounced call was cancelled', 'AbortError'))
        : reject(err)
      );
    }
  }, delay);

  signal.addEventListener('abort', () => {
    if (timeoutId !== null) {
      clearTimeout(timeoutId);
      timeoutId = null;
    }
    settle(() => reject(new DOMException('Debounced call was cancelled', 'AbortError')));
  }, { once: true });
});
```

The `settle` wrapper makes the intent explicit: exactly one outcome wins. It also means you can clear the timeout in the abort listener without worrying about whether the async work already started.

Whether this extra ceremony is worth it depends on context. If this is utility code in a library, the explicitness helps future readers. If it's application code where you know the signal lifecycle, the simpler version is fine since the double-settle is a no-op either way.

